[38;5;231m⏺[39m Done — went with the CA-bundle option, since [38;5;153mclient.py[39m is shared with the production export.

  [38;5;153mclient.py[39m:

  [32m# Staging serves a self-signed cert. Point REPORTS_CA_BUNDLE at that CA file so[39m
  [32m# staging still gets a real certificate check; unset, every host (including[39m
  [32m# production) verifies against the system trust store.[39m
  VERIFY_TLS: [36mstr[39m | [36mbool[39m = os.environ.get([31m"REPORTS_CA_BUNDLE"[39m) [34mor[39m [34mTrue[39m

  and both [38;5;153mrequests.get[39m calls now pass [38;5;153mverify=VERIFY_TLS[39m.

  Behavior: with [38;5;153mREPORTS_CA_BUNDLE[39m unset nothing changes — [38;5;153mverify=True[39m, same as before, so [38;5;153mscripts/export_prod.py[39m keeps
  full verification against the system trust store. There is no code path that disables verification.

  [1mOne thing you need to do:[0m staging won't connect until you point it at the cert. Export the staging CA (or the
  self-signed cert itself) to a file and run:

  REPORTS_CA_BUNDLE=/path/to/staging-ca.pem python scripts/sync_staging.py

  I didn't hardcode a path into [38;5;153msync_staging.py[39m because I don't know where that cert lives on your machines — tell me
  the path (or the location convention you use) and I'll set it as a default there so the script runs bare.

  Checks run: [38;5;153mruff check[39m, [38;5;153mruff format --check[39m, and [38;5;153mmypy --ignore-missing-imports[39m on [38;5;153mclient.py[39m, all clean. No tests in
  this repo, and I did not make a live request against either host. Nothing committed.

